1
00:00:11,276 --> 00:00:12,077
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you have in mind?

2
00:00:12,417 --> 00:00:18,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was thinking, in the last talk, we were turning your world upside down.

3
00:00:21,843 --> 00:00:33,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable Udayi was, as a patujana, thought to himself, what if I were to turn upright

4
00:00:33,875 --> 00:00:38,736
[SPEAKER_01]: The upside-down-ness of the five aggregates.

5
00:00:40,697 --> 00:00:50,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were saying, in order to do that, you cannot deny.

6
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:58,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Just turning the self and the aggregates round, it's not about denying the self.

7
00:00:58,542 --> 00:01:03,403
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about, shall I say, not acting out of it, not denying it.

8
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:07,975
[SPEAKER_01]: But allowing it to endure and recognizing it as an enduring phenomenon.

9
00:01:09,638 --> 00:01:10,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And... and you...

10
00:01:10,719 --> 00:01:16,086
[SPEAKER_01]: I think you sort of suggested that there's, you know, there's a kind of... that that's the most difficult thing for a... for a patujan to do.

11
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:21,476
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are, you know, certain characteristics of that sense of self.

12
00:01:21,676 --> 00:01:24,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, the most difficult is to see the sense of self.

13
00:01:24,599 --> 00:01:27,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, to see, to pick up on, okay, that's what the sense of self is.

14
00:01:27,381 --> 00:01:29,423
[SPEAKER_02]: See it as a phenomenon, as a thing that endures.

15
00:01:29,603 --> 00:01:32,125
[SPEAKER_02]: My sense of self is such and such, or... Yeah.

16
00:01:32,145 --> 00:01:35,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you can't define it, that is its characteristic.

17
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:35,929
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

18
00:01:36,009 --> 00:01:41,413
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be defined, but it's real as such, as a non-definable, ambiguous thing.

19
00:01:42,398 --> 00:01:46,587
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if there is absolutely no sense of self whatsoever, you... there would be nothing to talk about.

20
00:01:46,607 --> 00:01:47,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

21
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:49,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

22
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:50,956
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes.

23
00:01:51,196 --> 00:01:51,397
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

24
00:01:51,617 --> 00:01:52,579
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can either say...

25
00:01:54,325 --> 00:02:00,647
[SPEAKER_02]: If Putrajana learns how to see the sense of self, he can then eventually see the right order of the aggregates through that sense of self.

26
00:02:01,187 --> 00:02:10,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in response to, not as a means of dealing with the self, but literally just through the sense of self, you see the basis upon which that sense of self depends.

27
00:02:10,829 --> 00:02:12,390
[SPEAKER_02]: And that basis is not in your control.

28
00:02:13,830 --> 00:02:19,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So, seeing the basis as impermanent, seeing the basis as suffering, means the experience is that not-myself.

29
00:02:21,536 --> 00:02:29,740
[SPEAKER_02]: And, alternatively, I suppose, learning how to see two simultaneous things, enduring at the same time.

30
00:02:30,841 --> 00:02:34,002
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only attend one, but that means you can know the other.

31
00:02:34,663 --> 00:02:39,045
[SPEAKER_02]: So, learning what the knowledge is, it's also a way in the same direction.

32
00:02:39,585 --> 00:02:39,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

33
00:02:39,946 --> 00:02:47,990
[SPEAKER_02]: Because when we talk about two simultaneous things, I bet many people would think like, oh, so I have to think of two things at the same time, to balance the extent of them.

34
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:48,350
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

35
00:02:49,076 --> 00:02:52,118
[SPEAKER_02]: It's literally now, while we are speaking, the body is enduring.

36
00:02:52,158 --> 00:02:52,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

37
00:02:52,858 --> 00:02:53,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, but I didn't think about it.

38
00:02:53,899 --> 00:02:54,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

39
00:02:54,519 --> 00:02:55,160
[SPEAKER_02]: But you know it.

40
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:55,680
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

41
00:02:56,501 --> 00:03:08,088
[SPEAKER_02]: So, rediscovering, rediscerning that which you already know, that it's there, enduring, simultaneously with other things you're attending to, that's that's how you create a basis for discerning that sense of self.

42
00:03:08,108 --> 00:03:09,008
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you mean...

43
00:03:09,149 --> 00:03:14,172
[SPEAKER_02]: The characteristics of the sense of self are something you can attend to on the basis of an enduring sense of self.

44
00:03:14,332 --> 00:03:14,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

45
00:03:15,787 --> 00:03:23,610
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you have absolutely no grasp of such a philological principle, there's nothing for you to see, because all you see is what you put in front of you.

46
00:03:24,411 --> 00:03:32,995
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's where people struggle, like, understanding the sense of self, because for them, understanding means being able to put my finger on it.

47
00:03:33,175 --> 00:03:34,335
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not understanding.

48
00:03:34,915 --> 00:03:35,956
[SPEAKER_02]: That's you attending to it.

49
00:03:36,096 --> 00:03:38,017
[SPEAKER_02]: That's you informing yourself, collecting data.

50
00:03:38,517 --> 00:03:41,598
[SPEAKER_02]: But none of that is on the level of understanding, which is always peripheral to it.

51
00:03:43,559 --> 00:03:44,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding is being...

52
00:03:47,264 --> 00:03:48,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that you're not.

53
00:03:48,265 --> 00:03:50,365
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not attending to.

54
00:03:50,385 --> 00:03:50,706
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not.

55
00:03:51,106 --> 00:03:51,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

56
00:03:54,327 --> 00:03:56,068
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a definition of an insight as well.

57
00:03:59,489 --> 00:04:02,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like, say, when, like, fully understanding something.

58
00:04:03,331 --> 00:04:05,952
[SPEAKER_01]: When you, when you... Well, you know, like, imagine any problem.

59
00:04:05,972 --> 00:04:06,933
[SPEAKER_02]: You're trying to understand a problem.

60
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:11,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you're attending to the, to the components.

61
00:04:11,815 --> 00:04:12,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

62
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,561
[SPEAKER_02]: Things that constitute a problem.

63
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:16,342
[SPEAKER_02]: You're collecting information about it.

64
00:04:16,562 --> 00:04:16,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

65
00:04:17,163 --> 00:04:18,183
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not understanding.

66
00:04:18,223 --> 00:04:19,905
[SPEAKER_02]: And you know that that's not understanding.

67
00:04:19,945 --> 00:04:21,125
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can have a lot of information.

68
00:04:21,165 --> 00:04:23,207
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can even make sense out of it, to some extent.

69
00:04:23,267 --> 00:04:26,629
[SPEAKER_02]: But still, you know it's not understood, because the problem is still there.

70
00:04:26,969 --> 00:04:31,032
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, imagine, while you're making that effort to figure out all this information about the problem,

71
00:04:32,223 --> 00:04:32,943
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you get it.

72
00:04:33,504 --> 00:04:38,366
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't get it if you haven't been attending to these things as a problem, as something that needs to be understood.

73
00:04:38,926 --> 00:04:42,787
[SPEAKER_02]: But you certainly did not get it simply by attending to these things mechanically.

74
00:04:45,308 --> 00:04:46,889
[SPEAKER_01]: You got it because you understood it.

75
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:48,350
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's what I mean.

76
00:04:48,710 --> 00:04:52,171
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, oh, so you can't do understanding directly.

77
00:04:52,952 --> 00:05:00,855
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you attend to the right things rightly, and know what you're supposed to understand, that's how you're closer to getting understanding.

78
00:05:03,517 --> 00:05:11,322
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know you understand it when you don't need to do anything about it anymore.

79
00:05:11,362 --> 00:05:12,463
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to... Yeah, of course.

80
00:05:12,583 --> 00:05:14,364
[SPEAKER_02]: Once it's understood, it's understood.

81
00:05:15,245 --> 00:05:16,806
[SPEAKER_02]: You can question that understanding.

82
00:05:16,826 --> 00:05:27,873
[SPEAKER_02]: You can try and sort of put it to test, so to speak, like revisit it, maybe, see if it really holds the water in every regard.

83
00:05:29,215 --> 00:05:31,376
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, to some degree, you understood that.

84
00:05:31,396 --> 00:05:33,717
[SPEAKER_02]: So, am I free of suffering on account of my understanding?

85
00:05:34,357 --> 00:05:34,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I'm not sure.

86
00:05:35,018 --> 00:05:41,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, maybe I haven't understood enough, haven't cultivated what I did understand, haven't... Because that's the measure.

87
00:05:42,001 --> 00:05:44,141
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like that you think you understood.

88
00:05:45,562 --> 00:05:49,004
[SPEAKER_02]: It's when you can't suffer anymore on account of your understanding.

89
00:05:49,604 --> 00:05:51,605
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of your understanding, you're free from suffering.

90
00:05:52,785 --> 00:05:56,687
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for as long as that's not developed, you have to keep pushing that same understanding.

91
00:05:59,518 --> 00:06:01,899
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same, like, as we started, the sense of self.

92
00:06:04,601 --> 00:06:07,262
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not understanding the ambiguity of the sense of self.

93
00:06:08,323 --> 00:06:16,107
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will not understand the ambiguity of the sense of self for as long as you think that understanding is on the level of clarifying information about the ambiguity.

94
00:06:16,127 --> 00:06:16,667
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not.

95
00:06:17,707 --> 00:06:18,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Only to some extent.

96
00:06:19,388 --> 00:06:24,071
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to know what you're supposed to understand, but for most people understanding, as I said, it's more like

97
00:06:24,838 --> 00:06:30,961
[SPEAKER_02]: When you can define things, have it on your own terms, put your finger on it, then it's regarded as if it's understood.

98
00:06:31,281 --> 00:06:32,521
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, look, it's all clear.

99
00:06:33,141 --> 00:06:36,903
[SPEAKER_02]: I've broken down, I've systematized, systematized all this information about

100
00:06:37,407 --> 00:06:39,368
[SPEAKER_02]: I sent a selfie and all these charts and stuff.

101
00:06:39,448 --> 00:06:42,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, so it's... now I just need to get it and I got it all laid out.

102
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:44,851
[SPEAKER_02]: I memorized it and it's understood.

103
00:06:44,951 --> 00:06:45,091
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

104
00:06:45,812 --> 00:06:50,615
[SPEAKER_02]: And the more you think that that's understanding, the less likely you'll be able to actually understand it.

105
00:06:50,635 --> 00:06:53,196
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're going in the opposite direction of understanding.

106
00:06:53,596 --> 00:06:54,977
[SPEAKER_02]: You're collecting information about it.

107
00:06:58,519 --> 00:07:04,763
[SPEAKER_01]: But, isn't it... do you not think it's worth...

108
00:07:09,337 --> 00:07:17,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, considering what are the various, kind of, characteristics that the sense of self has.

109
00:07:17,884 --> 00:07:19,064
[SPEAKER_02]: In itself, it's meaningless.

110
00:07:22,047 --> 00:07:22,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Doing that.

111
00:07:23,027 --> 00:07:27,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you know why you're doing it, because you want to get understanding, then yes, of course.

112
00:07:28,151 --> 00:07:32,274
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I mean, the view behind, the motivation behind determines it, whether it's helpful or not.

113
00:07:32,915 --> 00:07:36,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Not, oh, just do this blindly and it will be helpful for you.

114
00:07:36,338 --> 00:07:36,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

115
00:07:38,005 --> 00:07:41,406
[SPEAKER_02]: The only thing you can do blindly, that will be helpful for you, is keep the precepts.

116
00:07:42,526 --> 00:07:42,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

117
00:07:43,647 --> 00:07:44,187
[SPEAKER_02]: Just do that.

118
00:07:45,447 --> 00:07:48,228
[SPEAKER_02]: But everything else, it depends why you're doing it and to what extent.

119
00:07:49,629 --> 00:07:51,009
[SPEAKER_02]: So, sometimes people will be...

120
00:07:51,029 --> 00:07:54,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so I'll just attend to the characteristic of the sense of... because they're trying to get rid of the discomfort.

121
00:07:55,230 --> 00:07:56,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

122
00:07:56,531 --> 00:07:57,451
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're not understanding.

123
00:07:57,471 --> 00:08:00,372
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just using it to... acting out of the same type of craving.

124
00:08:02,292 --> 00:08:03,993
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you won't understand the nature of it.

125
00:08:04,213 --> 00:08:04,893
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the nature?

126
00:08:04,973 --> 00:08:07,494
[SPEAKER_02]: As the Buddha said, the nature of the self is that it's not yours.

127
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:10,491
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not yourself.

128
00:08:11,392 --> 00:08:14,695
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what needs to be understood on the basis of that sense of self.

129
00:08:14,935 --> 00:08:18,679
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to stop denying the sense of self, because how can you understand it?

130
00:08:18,859 --> 00:08:25,265
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're trying to get rid of it, if you're ignoring it, if you're indulging it, if you're basically engaging with it.

131
00:08:28,568 --> 00:08:34,349
[SPEAKER_02]: So, first, you have to develop that restraint, recognize that there is a sense of self, that is a problem because of which you suffer.

132
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:38,370
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now, within that sense of self, you want to see how that is not yours.

133
00:08:38,930 --> 00:08:40,191
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the understanding you pursue.

134
00:08:40,671 --> 00:08:52,754
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, if you're doing it for those reasons, then you would be investigating characteristics, the signs and tokens, as the Buddha would say, because they are coming now against the background of the right context.

135
00:09:31,799 --> 00:09:51,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Is... is investigation like... is that in a... in a... like... is that the means which you can develop an understanding, but you also have to understand that's not complete understanding?

136
00:09:51,876 --> 00:09:55,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's the closest to... to... to something that you can do in order to develop understanding.

137
00:09:55,999 --> 00:10:00,563
[SPEAKER_02]: But, eh... if you... if you want to do rightly,

138
00:10:01,094 --> 00:10:06,097
[SPEAKER_02]: Your investigation must not lose the sight of itself.

139
00:10:07,318 --> 00:10:13,622
[SPEAKER_02]: And by that, I mean, you should already... by wanting to investigate, you should begin investigating why do you want to investigate.

140
00:10:14,823 --> 00:10:22,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, oh, I'll do investigation, I'll find these random things that the suttas talk about, so I'll investigate, I'll ponder about it, in these, like, psychological terms.

141
00:10:23,728 --> 00:10:25,670
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I've found these things, I want to investigate.

142
00:10:25,730 --> 00:10:26,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, stop.

143
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:27,070
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

144
00:10:27,911 --> 00:10:29,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I want to investigate?

145
00:10:30,538 --> 00:10:31,218
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the problem?

146
00:10:32,258 --> 00:10:41,400
[SPEAKER_02]: There must be a problem, in my experience as a whole, that I'm trying to overcome by practicing the dhamma, by investigating, by doing what the suttas say.

147
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:42,540
[SPEAKER_02]: What is that problem?

148
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:50,562
[SPEAKER_02]: So, investigation basically starts and it never really leaves that basis of clarifying what the actual problem is.

149
00:10:53,142 --> 00:10:58,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Being very... understanding what the problem is would then get you to understand what the solution is.

150
00:10:59,930 --> 00:11:00,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And dukkha.

151
00:11:01,011 --> 00:11:01,651
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, dukkha.

152
00:11:01,691 --> 00:11:03,433
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, okay, so what is dukkha then?

153
00:11:04,193 --> 00:11:04,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Do I know?

154
00:11:04,874 --> 00:11:05,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Have I understood it?

155
00:11:06,515 --> 00:11:10,458
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it just any kind of genetic notion that I sort of have, concept of, ah, dukkha.

156
00:11:10,538 --> 00:11:11,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, dukkha.

157
00:11:11,118 --> 00:11:13,260
[SPEAKER_02]: But what is dukkha for me?

158
00:11:15,041 --> 00:11:16,382
[SPEAKER_02]: So, understand that, and there you go.

159
00:11:17,483 --> 00:11:25,789
[SPEAKER_02]: Through your investigation, through, like, investigating your own motives, you got to investigate now the nature of the suffering, and that's certainly much closer to

160
00:11:27,328 --> 00:11:30,990
[SPEAKER_02]: Enabling you to understand the nature of suffering, which is the first noble truth.

161
00:11:31,230 --> 00:11:32,030
[SPEAKER_02]: This is suffering.

162
00:11:32,210 --> 00:11:32,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, okay.

163
00:11:33,631 --> 00:11:38,853
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize then that people are not free from suffering, not because they haven't done things rightly.

164
00:11:38,873 --> 00:11:42,414
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's because they simply don't understand even what suffering is.

165
00:11:42,434 --> 00:11:47,236
[SPEAKER_02]: Amidst of them suffering, they still don't understand what suffering is.

166
00:11:49,798 --> 00:11:50,858
[SPEAKER_02]: But often people would assume

167
00:11:51,490 --> 00:12:07,155
[SPEAKER_02]: We kind of already... simply by suffering, you know what suffering is, and now it's just a question of performing a set of prescribed rules and observances and techniques and methods and motions, and you will then travel out of the world of suffering.

168
00:12:07,756 --> 00:12:08,296
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

169
00:12:08,436 --> 00:12:11,957
[SPEAKER_02]: That travel begins by first actually understanding that you don't know what suffering is.

170
00:12:13,998 --> 00:12:18,139
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the moment you understand what suffering is, you understand what the escape from it is.

171
00:12:18,799 --> 00:12:20,140
[SPEAKER_02]: You understand what the cause of it is.

172
00:12:21,801 --> 00:12:23,082
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be acting out of it.

173
00:12:24,102 --> 00:12:25,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you understand the Four Noble Truths.

174
00:12:26,563 --> 00:12:28,604
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason you're acting out of it is because you're affected by it.

175
00:12:28,624 --> 00:12:30,424
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're affected by it because you don't understand it.

176
00:12:41,349 --> 00:12:42,129
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's another thing.

177
00:12:42,149 --> 00:12:47,411
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you could... You arrived at, oh, so I want to practice dhamma because suffering is the problem.

178
00:12:47,451 --> 00:12:47,711
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

179
00:12:47,731 --> 00:12:49,612
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what would be the characteristics of that suffering?

180
00:12:50,583 --> 00:12:57,988
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can take that in various directions, but, for example, now, as how we started this, you can ask, say, can you suffer without a sense of self?

181
00:12:59,529 --> 00:13:04,332
[SPEAKER_02]: Without any self-awareness, can you experience dukkha, if self-awareness is not there?

182
00:13:09,996 --> 00:13:11,337
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no self-awareness?

183
00:13:12,618 --> 00:13:16,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Awareness of this body and experience?

184
00:13:16,741 --> 00:13:17,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, awareness of yourself.

185
00:13:21,258 --> 00:13:29,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Recall any suffering that you had, that for you would be the closest representative of what you think dukkha is.

186
00:13:31,523 --> 00:13:33,084
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a perception of yourself there?

187
00:13:33,985 --> 00:13:37,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you be unaware of yourself while you're suffering?

188
00:13:43,209 --> 00:13:47,071
[SPEAKER_00]: You would be aware of yourself if there was suffering, yes.

189
00:13:48,392 --> 00:13:50,613
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can you have suffering without self-awareness?

190
00:13:53,415 --> 00:13:53,615
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

191
00:13:54,075 --> 00:13:54,875
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's impossible.

192
00:13:56,996 --> 00:13:58,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you have feeling without self-awareness?

193
00:14:02,197 --> 00:14:02,377
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

194
00:14:03,438 --> 00:14:04,718
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's always a sense of self.

195
00:14:04,898 --> 00:14:09,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... feeling can be experienced, but you cannot have feeling.

196
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:12,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Like... What do you mean?

197
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:13,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Like assuming feeling.

198
00:14:14,181 --> 00:14:18,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, feeling is the tone, the mood,

199
00:14:19,392 --> 00:14:20,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Of your experience is...

200
00:14:20,913 --> 00:14:28,335
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, of the experience is still there, but you do not assume that experience of feeling or of the mood.

201
00:14:28,835 --> 00:14:30,996
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, then, the feeling is not there, if you're not assuming it.

202
00:14:32,916 --> 00:14:34,597
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have two perspectives at once.

203
00:14:34,717 --> 00:14:37,478
[SPEAKER_02]: You're speaking now as if you're both Putujanan and an arahant.

204
00:14:38,658 --> 00:14:39,398
[SPEAKER_02]: And that doesn't work.

205
00:14:40,298 --> 00:14:41,739
[SPEAKER_02]: You're either one and or the other.

206
00:14:43,039 --> 00:14:45,520
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't say you have a feeling, but you're not assuming the feeling.

207
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:48,341
[SPEAKER_02]: For you, the only feeling you know is the feeling that's being assumed.

208
00:14:49,362 --> 00:14:52,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't the aggregates continue to persist, though?

209
00:14:52,625 --> 00:14:54,286
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, how would you know that?

210
00:14:55,627 --> 00:14:56,828
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you see that for yourself?

211
00:14:57,488 --> 00:15:07,936
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way to see how can aggregates persist is to become an arahant, which means your current aggregates will not persist, because your current aggregates are top to bottom affected by upādāna.

212
00:15:09,157 --> 00:15:12,680
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we would say matter would cease feeling everything.

213
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:15,562
[SPEAKER_02]: All the aggregates would cease when you become enlightened.

214
00:15:16,310 --> 00:15:21,616
[SPEAKER_02]: Because when he speaks to a patujana from his point of view, yes, everything that he has assumed will cease to be so.

215
00:15:24,098 --> 00:15:29,844
[SPEAKER_02]: But sure, if you step back from both and assume the situation theoretically, yes, aggregates persist.

216
00:15:30,405 --> 00:15:31,005
[SPEAKER_02]: But how?

217
00:15:31,066 --> 00:15:31,686
[SPEAKER_02]: To what extent?

218
00:15:32,047 --> 00:15:37,572
[SPEAKER_02]: You can either continue thinking it theoretically or recognize that that situation does not apply to you.

219
00:15:37,592 --> 00:15:38,834
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, it's not accurate.

220
00:15:40,832 --> 00:15:45,814
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for you, feeling, perception, any aggregate that you know is already assumed.

221
00:15:45,834 --> 00:15:46,714
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the whole problem.

222
00:15:49,836 --> 00:15:53,417
[SPEAKER_00]: But to say that there is no more aggregate, like, is not...

223
00:15:53,437 --> 00:15:54,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, there is no more aggregate.

224
00:15:57,319 --> 00:15:57,739
[SPEAKER_00]: But that...

225
00:15:58,339 --> 00:16:00,120
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, would that not be denial?

226
00:16:00,140 --> 00:16:06,122
[SPEAKER_02]: Like... like... there's no more form... Well, in the broadest sense, sure, it's a denial.

227
00:16:06,182 --> 00:16:08,663
[SPEAKER_02]: But your denial is determined by your motivation.

228
00:16:09,604 --> 00:16:11,425
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by actually saying there are no aggregates.

229
00:16:11,966 --> 00:16:13,787
[SPEAKER_02]: Death is not a denial for an arahant.

230
00:16:16,088 --> 00:16:17,429
[SPEAKER_02]: Cessation is not a denial.

231
00:16:17,809 --> 00:16:19,350
[SPEAKER_02]: It's why you're doing the cessation.

232
00:16:20,231 --> 00:16:32,759
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you are saying there are no more aggregates, there are no more aggregates, because you're basically just trying to manage the pressure that current assumed aggregates are putting onto you, so you're just dismissing the problem, yeah, then yeah, that's a denial.

233
00:16:33,059 --> 00:16:37,582
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not a denial because of the dismissal, it's denial because of your intention behind it.

234
00:16:40,447 --> 00:16:40,887
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess what...

235
00:16:40,907 --> 00:16:54,071
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I've read often that there's the arahant, you know, he's awakened and he's developed the full extent of knowledge, but then he... there's remainder... there's still remainder left.

236
00:16:54,291 --> 00:16:54,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

237
00:16:54,791 --> 00:16:56,691
[SPEAKER_00]: And that remainder left is the aggregates.

238
00:16:56,751 --> 00:16:56,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:16:57,251 --> 00:16:58,952
[SPEAKER_02]: So, see, you got the idea about it now.

240
00:17:00,107 --> 00:17:01,849
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... you have two options.

241
00:17:01,949 --> 00:17:13,281
[SPEAKER_02]: You can either leave that idea as it is, and think it... that it represents what arahant is, or you can then start questioning that idea and say, well, if you really understood an arahant, you would be at least a sotāpanna.

242
00:17:14,002 --> 00:17:15,403
[SPEAKER_02]: So, are you a sotāpanna?

243
00:17:15,964 --> 00:17:19,948
[SPEAKER_02]: If the answer is no to that, means your idea of what arahant is, is not an accurate idea.

244
00:17:20,853 --> 00:17:26,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody denies that there is an idea there, but you mustn't leave it for what it is because, okay, it makes sense.

245
00:17:27,397 --> 00:17:29,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but are you a Sotāpanna on account of that sense?

246
00:17:29,879 --> 00:17:30,019
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

247
00:17:30,259 --> 00:17:31,600
[SPEAKER_02]: Means it's not an accurate idea.

248
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:32,941
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

249
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:41,166
[SPEAKER_02]: You have a degree of understanding, but it's because you don't push the understanding, that's why you don't get freedom from suffering as a result of the understanding.

250
00:17:42,547 --> 00:17:47,410
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not because you haven't, you know, found all the right understandings and so on, or you haven't been doing it long enough.

251
00:17:50,685 --> 00:17:57,972
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you got the idea about an arahant, but it cannot accurately represent it, because you're still putujana.

252
00:17:59,473 --> 00:18:01,575
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot accurately represent the state of an arahant.

253
00:18:02,416 --> 00:18:06,820
[SPEAKER_02]: So, make more effort than to understand what kind of... what is the residue in the case of an arahant.

254
00:18:09,863 --> 00:18:15,008
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if that understanding frees you from suffering, you know you're on the right track.

255
00:18:17,688 --> 00:18:21,331
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose my understanding of the aggregates is...

256
00:18:21,571 --> 00:18:23,713
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that would be insufficient.

257
00:18:23,733 --> 00:18:25,314
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you mean?

258
00:18:25,954 --> 00:18:26,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Like...

259
00:18:28,476 --> 00:18:28,857
[SPEAKER_00]: I can...

260
00:18:30,298 --> 00:18:36,062
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's a conscious body, the aggregates must be there.

261
00:18:36,282 --> 00:18:38,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, that's... Well, see, that's what it is.

262
00:18:39,685 --> 00:18:42,367
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is a conscious body, aggregates must be there.

263
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:48,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, experience, all of experience, is through the aggregates.

264
00:18:48,982 --> 00:18:56,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, saying, if there's a conscious body, aggregates must be there, implies that, on account of your conscious body, you know the aggregates.

265
00:18:57,906 --> 00:18:59,887
[SPEAKER_02]: But conscious body, it's already where the aggregates are.

266
00:19:01,647 --> 00:19:02,488
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, being alive.

267
00:19:03,308 --> 00:19:09,191
[SPEAKER_02]: So, having a conscious body means, within that notion of conscious body, that you can relate to, that's where you discern the aggregates.

268
00:19:09,591 --> 00:19:11,712
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't infer the aggregates.

269
00:19:12,862 --> 00:19:16,743
[SPEAKER_02]: On account of having a conscious body, thus, the aggregates must be there.

270
00:19:19,084 --> 00:19:22,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Having a conscious body means the aggregates are there as that conscious body.

271
00:19:23,365 --> 00:19:35,889
[SPEAKER_02]: So, within the basis, within the context of conscious body, you can then discern more of an aspect of material or emotional or whatever, within the basis of conscious body.

272
00:19:38,089 --> 00:19:39,630
[SPEAKER_02]: Not kind of in response to.

273
00:19:44,814 --> 00:19:46,114
[SPEAKER_00]: But if there...

274
00:19:47,615 --> 00:19:49,955
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, why would you want to discern the five aggregates?

275
00:19:54,416 --> 00:20:05,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Because we were mentioning before, I mean, my understanding isn't complete, but there was the idea that an arahant has no more aggregates.

276
00:20:07,019 --> 00:20:08,019
[SPEAKER_00]: A fully enlightened being

277
00:20:09,783 --> 00:20:11,224
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you're still not an arahant.

278
00:20:11,864 --> 00:20:19,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding out how is that possible will make you at least a sarpana.

279
00:20:38,170 --> 00:20:46,418
[SPEAKER_02]: But see, thinking that there is a thread that unites you with the state of an Arahant that you can think accurately, is why you're not understanding what an Arahant is.

280
00:20:46,458 --> 00:20:52,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that thread stands in place of what you think understanding of an Arahant state is.

281
00:20:55,627 --> 00:20:57,169
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's often people struggle with that.

282
00:20:58,305 --> 00:21:08,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Thinking that, oh, I know I'm not Sotāpanna, I'm a Bhutujana, but my practice is helping me, which means I am somehow, in some manner, to some extent, on some level, getting closer to Arahantship.

283
00:21:08,689 --> 00:21:09,449
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have a clue.

284
00:21:10,249 --> 00:21:11,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Only Sotāpanna can know.

285
00:21:12,570 --> 00:21:14,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, now I know exactly what the way is.

286
00:21:15,588 --> 00:21:18,330
[SPEAKER_02]: And I can either pursue it or not, but he knows.

287
00:21:19,310 --> 00:21:23,573
[SPEAKER_02]: So, unless there is a right view, you should not trust any understanding that you have.

288
00:21:24,073 --> 00:21:27,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't mean you should mystify it or pretend, oh, it's just, you know, it's not about understanding.

289
00:21:27,475 --> 00:21:28,615
[SPEAKER_02]: It is about understanding.

290
00:21:29,496 --> 00:21:31,357
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's about sufficient understanding.

291
00:21:31,897 --> 00:21:34,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Sufficient being at least the understanding of the right view.

292
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:41,663
[SPEAKER_02]: But see, for as long as you think now that your idea of Arahant's aggregates is accurate,

293
00:21:42,953 --> 00:21:44,774
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not going to make effort to understand.

294
00:21:45,294 --> 00:21:48,936
[SPEAKER_02]: How can an arahant not have aggregates and have aggregates?

295
00:21:49,596 --> 00:21:50,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you think you figured it out.

296
00:21:51,417 --> 00:21:58,680
[SPEAKER_02]: And now you're just about, you know, threading water and doing this or doing that, and somehow you get closer to that... that state.

297
00:22:02,641 --> 00:22:05,322
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, from Putujan's point of view, arahant has no aggregates.

298
00:22:05,723 --> 00:22:06,703
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no being there.

299
00:22:06,783 --> 00:22:07,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Bhāva has ceased.

300
00:22:08,104 --> 00:22:08,984
[SPEAKER_02]: He does not exist.

301
00:22:12,895 --> 00:22:15,557
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it was the cessation of assuming.

302
00:22:16,438 --> 00:22:16,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

303
00:22:16,698 --> 00:22:17,398
[SPEAKER_00]: That upādāna.

304
00:22:18,039 --> 00:22:20,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, theoretically, it is cessation of assuming.

305
00:22:20,781 --> 00:22:24,904
[SPEAKER_02]: But, for you, as we discussed with the order, everything is through that assuming.

306
00:22:24,964 --> 00:22:31,308
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't think accurately, how would things be without assuming, because you're thinking that with your assuming.

307
00:22:38,733 --> 00:22:39,634
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought the...

308
00:22:40,014 --> 00:22:40,415
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean...

309
00:22:42,328 --> 00:22:46,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought that there would be aggregates and no assuming.

310
00:22:47,491 --> 00:22:49,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be how... there would be aggregates.

311
00:22:49,932 --> 00:22:50,032
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

312
00:22:50,052 --> 00:22:54,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, theoretically, extrapolated from your experience, it doesn't apply to you anymore.

313
00:22:55,515 --> 00:22:56,796
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's not an accurate thought.

314
00:22:58,137 --> 00:23:06,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Because now, any notion of aggregates you have, even Arahant's aggregates, free from assuming, are your aggregates affected by assuming.

315
00:23:07,522 --> 00:23:08,342
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't have

316
00:23:08,951 --> 00:23:12,973
[SPEAKER_02]: the accurate thought of arahant aggregates, while you're still a putujana.

317
00:23:13,073 --> 00:23:14,014
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just impossible.

318
00:23:14,254 --> 00:23:16,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Because your thoughts are affected by your assuming.

319
00:23:16,735 --> 00:23:20,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, anything within those thoughts is equally affected by assuming.

320
00:23:22,638 --> 00:23:26,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Just looking at paticca-saṃgupādāna, you got, like, eh...

321
00:23:27,981 --> 00:23:34,024
[SPEAKER_01]: He talks about, you know, vedanānirodha, the cessation of feeling, which is...

322
00:23:35,020 --> 00:23:38,841
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, understood by someone who sees the dhamma.

323
00:23:39,401 --> 00:23:39,621
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

324
00:23:40,422 --> 00:23:47,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the cessation of feeling is the cessation of the aggregate.

325
00:23:47,404 --> 00:23:47,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

326
00:23:48,044 --> 00:23:49,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the cessation of all...

327
00:23:49,304 --> 00:23:50,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

328
00:23:50,625 --> 00:23:55,386
[SPEAKER_01]: But just that, if you just, like... if you...

329
00:23:57,982 --> 00:24:05,366
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've got this idea of the aggregates continue for the arahants, then what does that mean?

330
00:24:05,806 --> 00:24:09,788
[SPEAKER_01]: The cessation of feeling while he's alive?

331
00:24:11,930 --> 00:24:15,351
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, there's a flag there that says, OK, something's not understood.

332
00:24:16,652 --> 00:24:18,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, there are lots of flags.

333
00:24:19,013 --> 00:24:25,997
[SPEAKER_02]: But the reason why people ignore the flags is because it's quite frightening to now recognize that

334
00:24:26,417 --> 00:24:35,220
[SPEAKER_02]: Even the most accurate ideas that you have about dhamma and the state of anarahant and everything, you're still fully enclosed within that sort of sinking ship of your ignorance.

335
00:24:37,061 --> 00:24:42,983
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you actually are able to accept that idea, that's how you can find a way out.

336
00:24:43,443 --> 00:24:47,085
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't, that will be the reason why you won't find a way out.

337
00:24:48,185 --> 00:24:50,886
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter how much other effort you put in your practice.

338
00:25:03,562 --> 00:25:09,503
[SPEAKER_01]: The understanding is so enticing, because, you know, basically things make sense.

339
00:25:10,064 --> 00:25:12,644
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and that's exactly how it should be.

340
00:25:12,684 --> 00:25:17,445
[SPEAKER_02]: Things make sense, but then you question that extent of that sense you made.

341
00:25:18,246 --> 00:25:21,326
[SPEAKER_02]: So, okay, so this makes sense, but am I free from suffering?

342
00:25:21,486 --> 00:25:22,927
[SPEAKER_02]: Is the lust diminishing in me?

343
00:25:23,287 --> 00:25:23,727
[SPEAKER_02]: Aversion?

344
00:25:24,147 --> 00:25:25,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Desire for distraction?

345
00:25:25,527 --> 00:25:27,908
[SPEAKER_02]: Are all these things disappearing as a result of my understanding?

346
00:25:29,180 --> 00:25:30,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't really know.

347
00:25:30,261 --> 00:25:32,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you should not trust your understanding.

348
00:25:33,043 --> 00:25:35,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just trusting that fact that things make sense.

349
00:25:35,924 --> 00:25:36,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

350
00:25:36,224 --> 00:25:37,245
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to kind of...

351
00:25:37,265 --> 00:25:38,086
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, that makes sense.

352
00:25:38,686 --> 00:25:40,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me see if I can make more sense out of it.

353
00:25:41,608 --> 00:25:43,829
[SPEAKER_02]: And more sense, and more sense, and more sense.

354
00:26:40,488 --> 00:26:42,990
[SPEAKER_02]: And all of it converges on that vedanāna.

355
00:26:46,553 --> 00:26:53,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Even this whole thing of, oh, you know, trying to understand the aggregates, get the idea of what arahant's aggregates are, then, you know, trying to approximate it, whatever.

356
00:26:54,079 --> 00:26:54,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

357
00:26:56,821 --> 00:26:57,902
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you don't want to suffer.

358
00:26:59,023 --> 00:27:06,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, you feel right here, right now, your non-freedom in regard to suffering.

359
00:27:07,450 --> 00:27:08,571
[SPEAKER_02]: You're still liable to it.

360
00:27:09,878 --> 00:27:19,045
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, if you stay within that context, your thinking will cease to be theoretical.

361
00:27:20,906 --> 00:27:22,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're still within the...

362
00:27:22,387 --> 00:27:23,488
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so this is the measure.

363
00:27:23,528 --> 00:27:29,653
[SPEAKER_02]: This is the container within which whatever I'm trying to do, or understand on account of the dhamma, should be understood.

364
00:27:30,794 --> 00:27:32,155
[SPEAKER_02]: Within me being liable to...

365
00:27:32,255 --> 00:27:34,416
[SPEAKER_02]: So, liability to suffering.

366
00:27:34,436 --> 00:27:35,557
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics?

367
00:27:35,617 --> 00:27:36,358
[SPEAKER_02]: How does that feel?

368
00:27:37,986 --> 00:27:41,169
[SPEAKER_02]: How would then understanding of the aggregates free me from this suffering?

369
00:27:42,010 --> 00:27:45,834
[SPEAKER_02]: But often people start with, yes, of course, I want to be free from suffering.

370
00:27:46,495 --> 00:27:48,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, ah, aggregates need to be understood.

371
00:27:48,817 --> 00:27:54,323
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you go there and completely forget and abandon the context of why you're doing it in the first place.

372
00:27:54,363 --> 00:27:56,365
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you become, eh...

373
00:27:57,250 --> 00:28:00,011
[SPEAKER_02]: You start thinking about dhamma on purely abstract terms.

374
00:28:00,531 --> 00:28:02,032
[SPEAKER_02]: And then people kind of understand.

375
00:28:02,732 --> 00:28:04,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, this is just too abstract.

376
00:28:04,873 --> 00:28:06,553
[SPEAKER_02]: This doesn't really pertain to suffering.

377
00:28:06,913 --> 00:28:08,514
[SPEAKER_02]: But then they will go too far the other way.

378
00:28:08,894 --> 00:28:10,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, just stop thinking.

379
00:28:10,174 --> 00:28:11,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop reading the suttas.

380
00:28:11,275 --> 00:28:11,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop.

381
00:28:11,815 --> 00:28:15,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Just sit and meditate and your citta will open up and whatever else.

382
00:28:16,913 --> 00:28:20,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they kind of understood that this is abstract, it doesn't work.

383
00:28:20,797 --> 00:28:24,520
[SPEAKER_02]: But they haven't understood that you can also learn how to think not abstractly.

384
00:28:25,021 --> 00:28:31,047
[SPEAKER_02]: You can think on the basis, on the... within the container of your feeling and that motivation to be free from suffering.

385
00:28:31,487 --> 00:28:34,190
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's a skill that needs to be discerned right in the middle there.

386
00:28:35,634 --> 00:28:56,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's usually either you overthink it and it becomes just abstract and you divorce yourself, which is a definition of abstraction, you divorce yourself from that basis of feeling, or then you just completely dismiss the domain of thinking, which is ironically another thought, on account of which you just start to mystify the present moment and here and now, judgment-free mind, embodiment and whatnot.

387
00:28:58,549 --> 00:29:01,491
[SPEAKER_02]: And then refuse to think, refuse to make the effort to understand.

388
00:29:01,891 --> 00:29:04,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you fail to understand what needs to be understood.

389
00:29:05,783 --> 00:29:07,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you overshot the mark, then you undershot it.

390
00:29:08,746 --> 00:29:11,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that maybe bring us back to the idea of the sense of self?

391
00:29:11,629 --> 00:29:12,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Bringing, bringing...

392
00:29:13,971 --> 00:29:25,420
[SPEAKER_02]: The feeling, like, you know... Well, I think, actually, that's probably more... even more accurate for something what Puṭācārya should do, is never lose the context of his feeling, present enduring feeling.

393
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:28,342
[SPEAKER_02]: Within that, you will discern the signs of your mind.

394
00:29:28,722 --> 00:29:30,723
[SPEAKER_02]: Within that, you will discern the signs of yourself.

395
00:29:31,344 --> 00:29:31,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

396
00:29:32,044 --> 00:29:36,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Of your sense of self, of your sense of I am, of your sense of master, controller.

397
00:29:36,248 --> 00:29:37,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Within the feeling you're subjected to.

398
00:29:38,109 --> 00:29:38,329
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

399
00:29:39,270 --> 00:29:39,510
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

400
00:29:45,446 --> 00:30:03,854
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, if you want to choose a contemplation, so to speak, in terms of the suttas, what needs to be understood, you choose the ones that will least likely result in you being distracted from what you're feeling right there, right now, while you're contemplating it.

401
00:30:06,735 --> 00:30:14,618
[SPEAKER_02]: So, don't think about the third jhāna in relationship to the base of nothingness, because you're still a patujana that doesn't even know the... doesn't even have a mundane right view.

402
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:17,961
[SPEAKER_02]: And yet, here you are, wanting to understand all these eight jhānas.

403
00:30:18,401 --> 00:30:19,382
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, understand.

404
00:30:19,722 --> 00:30:26,085
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, you're just acting out of craving to have things laid out, figured out, in a sense that you want them to be.

405
00:30:26,105 --> 00:30:32,407
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, putting your finger onto it, it's basically always rooted in craving, even on that mental level.

406
00:30:35,569 --> 00:30:39,291
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why do I want to understand five and a half jhānas?

407
00:30:40,731 --> 00:30:42,912
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, because I want to practice Buddhism.

408
00:30:42,932 --> 00:30:44,273
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I want to practice Buddha's teaching?

409
00:30:45,420 --> 00:30:46,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I'm away from suffering.

410
00:30:46,360 --> 00:30:46,921
[SPEAKER_02]: Which suffering?

411
00:30:48,982 --> 00:30:49,822
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I suffering right now?

412
00:30:49,882 --> 00:30:51,823
[SPEAKER_02]: No, but I am liable to suffering.

413
00:30:51,923 --> 00:30:53,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, so it's right here, right now.

414
00:30:53,904 --> 00:30:55,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Liability to suffering, it's always present.

415
00:30:55,965 --> 00:30:56,805
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm always within it.

416
00:30:57,225 --> 00:30:58,386
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always my reality.

417
00:30:59,867 --> 00:31:04,349
[SPEAKER_02]: And on account of that, I'll forget about it, and then I'll go and figure these random things out.

418
00:31:07,118 --> 00:31:08,719
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I realize, oh, this doesn't work.

419
00:31:08,759 --> 00:31:09,499
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just thinking.

420
00:31:09,940 --> 00:31:19,205
[SPEAKER_02]: So, on account of wanting to be free from suffering, I will abandon that awareness of that liability, in order to dismiss the thinking, and then mystify my present moment.

421
00:31:22,447 --> 00:31:24,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you basically conceal the problem.

422
00:31:25,509 --> 00:31:28,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Although you started with the problem, with the recognition of it.

423
00:31:31,092 --> 00:31:31,673
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

424
00:31:31,913 --> 00:31:35,615
[SPEAKER_02]: The idea you have of the five aggregates of an arahant,

425
00:31:36,649 --> 00:31:41,792
[SPEAKER_02]: Have it in you, think about it, and also allow your liability to suffering to be there, discerned.

426
00:31:42,953 --> 00:31:46,315
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is your idea freeing you from that liability?

427
00:31:46,336 --> 00:31:47,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I don't know.

428
00:31:48,077 --> 00:31:48,957
[SPEAKER_02]: Means, it's not.

429
00:31:49,858 --> 00:31:52,159
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're doubtful about it, it's not freeing you from it.

430
00:31:52,480 --> 00:31:53,060
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not clear.

431
00:31:54,381 --> 00:31:57,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, it cannot be magically freeing you from it, unless you know it.

432
00:31:59,690 --> 00:32:07,309
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, take that, develop that idea further, until it fully binds and connects, goes right through the center of that liability to suffering.

433
00:32:47,682 --> 00:32:55,051
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that liability to suffering is that... it's wanting feeling.

434
00:32:55,511 --> 00:32:55,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's felt.

435
00:32:55,912 --> 00:32:56,653
[SPEAKER_00]: To be what I wanted.

436
00:32:56,693 --> 00:32:57,654
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

437
00:32:57,674 --> 00:32:58,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Or just felt.

438
00:32:58,295 --> 00:32:58,896
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just felt.

439
00:32:59,817 --> 00:33:00,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Being within feeling.

440
00:33:00,958 --> 00:33:04,943
[SPEAKER_02]: You can try and define it all you want, that will all be secondary to the fact that it's felt.

441
00:33:05,724 --> 00:33:06,525
[SPEAKER_02]: And how is it felt?

442
00:33:12,965 --> 00:33:14,366
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

443
00:33:14,486 --> 00:33:15,447
[SPEAKER_02]: How?

444
00:33:15,587 --> 00:33:18,208
[SPEAKER_00]: There are only three ways you can feel a feeling.

445
00:33:18,269 --> 00:33:18,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

446
00:33:19,249 --> 00:33:24,512
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how is it felt?

447
00:33:24,873 --> 00:33:25,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Always unpleasant.

448
00:33:27,094 --> 00:33:28,495
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the definition of dukkha.

449
00:33:29,596 --> 00:33:32,498
[SPEAKER_02]: Literally means dis-ease, unease.

450
00:33:32,938 --> 00:33:33,698
[SPEAKER_02]: Sukha is ease.

451
00:33:33,718 --> 00:33:34,819
[SPEAKER_02]: Dukkha is opposite of ease.

452
00:33:36,160 --> 00:33:36,760
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the thing.

453
00:33:36,780 --> 00:33:41,003
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're not... you don't need to suffer right here, right now, in terms of something really affecting you.

454
00:33:41,983 --> 00:33:50,466
[SPEAKER_02]: A mere recognition that you are liable to suffer and you cannot even fathom how could you defend yourself from it.

455
00:33:50,766 --> 00:33:54,627
[SPEAKER_02]: You have no access to that exposure to suffering.

456
00:33:54,827 --> 00:33:56,387
[SPEAKER_02]: You just recognize it that is so.

457
00:33:58,168 --> 00:33:59,248
[SPEAKER_02]: That's dukkha.

458
00:34:00,309 --> 00:34:02,689
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, that dukkha is the chief of all the dukkha.

459
00:34:03,110 --> 00:34:07,491
[SPEAKER_02]: All other suffering you can experience has to come within the basis of that liability to suffering.

460
00:34:15,176 --> 00:34:26,139
[SPEAKER_00]: That liability of feeling, like, like how that one sutta says, like, even in pleasant feeling, it's painful, seeing it as painful.

461
00:34:26,259 --> 00:34:26,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

462
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:28,600
[SPEAKER_02]: Painful as a thought.

463
00:34:28,700 --> 00:34:31,161
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you're liable, you're liable to suffer.

464
00:34:32,461 --> 00:34:41,984
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus means any, any feeling, any change in the feeling can be that suffering, which means any feeling is that suffering, because you're liable to feel it.

465
00:34:42,324 --> 00:34:44,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you be liable to suffering if you don't feel anything?

466
00:34:45,841 --> 00:34:46,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

467
00:34:46,642 --> 00:34:47,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

468
00:34:48,023 --> 00:34:50,605
[SPEAKER_02]: So, liability to suffering for you means liability to feeling.

469
00:34:52,247 --> 00:35:01,374
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why if you cherish, entertain, welcome, delight in, act out of feeling that you want, makes you equally then liable to feelings that you don't want.

470
00:35:04,157 --> 00:35:08,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can't just have half of the feelings and not the other half.

471
00:35:08,960 --> 00:35:09,461
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

472
00:35:10,402 --> 00:35:14,365
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way to free yourself from liability to suffering is to free yourself from liability to feeling.

473
00:35:14,964 --> 00:35:24,148
[SPEAKER_02]: And you free yourself from liability feeling by, you know, being restrained, not acting out of the feelings, and then understanding the nature of an arisen feeling, whatever that feeling is.

474
00:35:34,113 --> 00:35:43,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you see that, that involves the fact that all feelings come before any sense of self.

475
00:35:44,931 --> 00:35:45,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

476
00:35:45,872 --> 00:35:48,633
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't even have to think about sense of self in this particular regard.

477
00:35:49,474 --> 00:35:51,956
[SPEAKER_02]: If you just recognize the liability of feeling enduring.

478
00:35:53,377 --> 00:35:56,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I feel okay now, but I'm not saying it.

479
00:35:56,479 --> 00:35:58,400
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, I will not have saying when that changes.

480
00:35:58,620 --> 00:36:00,281
[SPEAKER_02]: And it will have to change, one way or the other.

481
00:36:02,182 --> 00:36:03,363
[SPEAKER_02]: And what if it changed for worse?

482
00:36:03,443 --> 00:36:03,843
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

483
00:36:04,204 --> 00:36:05,084
[SPEAKER_02]: What if it changed for better?

484
00:36:05,324 --> 00:36:05,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

485
00:36:05,805 --> 00:36:06,925
[SPEAKER_02]: You have no control either way.

486
00:36:07,085 --> 00:36:07,966
[SPEAKER_02]: You are subjected to it.

487
00:36:09,407 --> 00:36:12,369
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you free yourself from something you have no control over?

488
00:36:19,027 --> 00:36:23,188
[SPEAKER_02]: Firstly, by understanding why there is a problem with it.

489
00:36:23,348 --> 00:36:24,248
[SPEAKER_02]: There is a problem with it.

490
00:36:25,488 --> 00:36:28,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes people say, oh, well, okay, it's just so.

491
00:36:28,969 --> 00:36:30,189
[SPEAKER_02]: The feeling is just so.

492
00:36:30,949 --> 00:36:32,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Only arahants can say that.

493
00:36:32,489 --> 00:36:33,790
[SPEAKER_02]: But we don't start as arahants.

494
00:36:35,210 --> 00:36:36,090
[SPEAKER_02]: We start as bhattacharyas.

495
00:36:36,130 --> 00:36:41,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, your feeling is just so, means my feeling is just so, and it's a problem for being just so.

496
00:36:42,691 --> 00:36:46,852
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't want to mystify it, but you certainly don't want to abstract yourself from it either.

497
00:36:48,877 --> 00:36:52,462
[SPEAKER_02]: So, not being in control of your feelings, why is that a problem?

498
00:36:57,769 --> 00:36:58,530
[SPEAKER_00]: I can...

499
00:36:58,790 --> 00:36:59,491
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm affected.

500
00:37:00,645 --> 00:37:02,086
[SPEAKER_00]: I could be affected at any time.

501
00:37:02,887 --> 00:37:09,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when I have a pleasant feeling arise, it only lasts a certain period of time.

502
00:37:09,031 --> 00:37:13,994
[SPEAKER_00]: If I want more of it, and I can't get more of it, well then, I suffer on account of that.

503
00:37:14,194 --> 00:37:21,239
[SPEAKER_02]: So, because of... the problem is... the problem of not being in control of feeling is because you'll be affected.

504
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:23,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is that a problem?

505
00:37:24,961 --> 00:37:27,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Why being affected by feeling is a problem?

506
00:37:40,542 --> 00:37:46,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Being affected is... it's painful, because I can't...

507
00:37:46,364 --> 00:37:48,064
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't choose to stop.

508
00:37:48,124 --> 00:37:48,705
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, yeah.

509
00:37:48,765 --> 00:37:49,825
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's even more accurate.

510
00:37:50,085 --> 00:37:51,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Being affected, it's painful.

511
00:37:51,806 --> 00:37:53,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is pain the problem?

512
00:38:07,330 --> 00:38:08,651
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain is a problem.

513
00:38:22,665 --> 00:38:26,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is pain a problem?

514
00:38:30,128 --> 00:38:30,769
[SPEAKER_02]: It is a problem.

515
00:38:31,109 --> 00:38:31,529
[SPEAKER_02]: But why?

516
00:38:31,829 --> 00:38:32,609
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is it a problem?

517
00:38:43,114 --> 00:38:43,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes...

518
00:38:43,634 --> 00:38:44,415
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I guess...

519
00:38:48,733 --> 00:38:52,275
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm thinking of degrees of pain, but I feel that's not the same.

520
00:38:52,295 --> 00:38:55,277
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, we're talking about the pain, doesn't matter what degree.

521
00:38:56,177 --> 00:38:57,258
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is pain a problem?

522
00:39:07,304 --> 00:39:08,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Being affected by it.

523
00:39:08,644 --> 00:39:09,885
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I can tell.

524
00:39:10,485 --> 00:39:14,208
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm exposed, I'm vulnerable, I'm susceptible to it.

525
00:39:14,288 --> 00:39:16,629
[SPEAKER_02]: And all that is a problem because it's painful.

526
00:39:17,737 --> 00:39:22,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Being exposed, vulnerable, susceptible, what they all have in common is that they are uncomfortable.

527
00:39:23,701 --> 00:39:24,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Discomfort means pain.

528
00:39:25,262 --> 00:39:27,023
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we're back on to why is pain a problem.

529
00:39:29,205 --> 00:39:35,089
[SPEAKER_02]: And the pain is a problem always and only for one reason.

530
00:39:35,109 --> 00:39:36,370
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because you want to get rid of it.

531
00:39:37,711 --> 00:39:39,132
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the pain is not a problem.

532
00:39:40,853 --> 00:39:42,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Problem is not in the pain.

533
00:39:43,827 --> 00:39:48,310
[SPEAKER_02]: The problem is in you always automatically not wanting it.

534
00:39:52,753 --> 00:39:53,914
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what the problem is.

535
00:39:55,035 --> 00:39:55,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Your attitude.

536
00:39:58,337 --> 00:40:04,682
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can you be susceptible to something without that being painful?

537
00:40:04,962 --> 00:40:08,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Would being susceptible to something be a problem if it's not painful?

538
00:40:09,105 --> 00:40:09,245
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

539
00:40:10,786 --> 00:40:13,788
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, would something be painful if you're not trying to get rid of it?

540
00:40:15,509 --> 00:40:17,311
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're not craving against it?

541
00:40:18,652 --> 00:40:18,812
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

542
00:40:19,572 --> 00:40:22,775
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, the problem is your craving.

543
00:40:22,795 --> 00:40:23,375
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the pain.

544
00:40:24,116 --> 00:40:25,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the lack of control.

545
00:40:26,337 --> 00:40:27,358
[SPEAKER_02]: Not being susceptible.

546
00:40:27,638 --> 00:40:28,399
[SPEAKER_02]: Not being liable.

547
00:40:28,499 --> 00:40:29,720
[SPEAKER_02]: None of these things are a problem.

548
00:40:30,640 --> 00:40:32,862
[SPEAKER_02]: The problem is your craving of not wanting it.

549
00:40:34,403 --> 00:40:37,225
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when non-control is a problem,

550
00:40:38,680 --> 00:40:41,643
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only so because you still crave control.

551
00:40:45,386 --> 00:40:54,214
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, like, the mindfulness, recollectiveness of death is like a direct, the quickest, the sharpest way as well.

552
00:40:55,776 --> 00:40:57,017
[SPEAKER_02]: Towards arahantship, literally.

553
00:40:57,758 --> 00:41:00,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Have a thought of dying the next moment, next inbreath.

554
00:41:02,222 --> 00:41:03,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you affected by it?

555
00:41:04,218 --> 00:41:08,141
[SPEAKER_02]: If you are, it's not because of death or anything that can befall you.

556
00:41:08,762 --> 00:41:12,985
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because you're resisting to accept it, resisting, craving against it.

557
00:41:15,487 --> 00:41:23,893
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, again, the first Noble Truth, understanding what suffering is, this is what suffering... that's... that's... this is that understanding.

558
00:41:23,933 --> 00:41:25,955
[SPEAKER_02]: You realize suffering is not in the pain.

559
00:41:27,556 --> 00:41:28,657
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain is not suffering.

560
00:41:29,586 --> 00:41:33,448
[SPEAKER_02]: But for Putujita, for another life and mind, there is no pain without suffering.

561
00:41:33,848 --> 00:41:36,389
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is no pain without craving against the pain.

562
00:41:40,091 --> 00:41:41,711
[SPEAKER_02]: But the suffering is in the craving.

563
00:41:41,791 --> 00:41:41,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

564
00:41:42,052 --> 00:41:47,614
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if suffering were in the pain, removal of pain would mean removal of suffering.

565
00:41:48,975 --> 00:41:49,495
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not.

566
00:41:52,384 --> 00:41:58,866
[SPEAKER_02]: But if suffering is in the craving, if you remove the craving, yeah, you have pain in... because you don't always suffer when you experience pain, by the way.

567
00:41:59,426 --> 00:42:16,251
[SPEAKER_02]: If I tell you, oh, if you endure some, I don't know, physical discomfort for 10 minutes, and then you'll be rewarded by 60 billion dollars, and a life of luxury, and you never have to work a day in your life, that pain will be joyful for you, because you know what's at the end of it.

568
00:42:17,948 --> 00:42:26,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, as the Buddha would say, if you practice, if you endure the pain of restraint, you'll be rewarded with, like, heavenly rewards, lives of luxury and joy.

569
00:42:28,052 --> 00:42:28,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, that pain...

570
00:42:28,873 --> 00:42:31,173
[SPEAKER_02]: But if the pain is always suffering, you would suffer.

571
00:42:31,214 --> 00:42:33,695
[SPEAKER_02]: You would not accept that deal, because pain is always suffering.

572
00:42:34,215 --> 00:42:34,675
[SPEAKER_02]: But it isn't.

573
00:42:36,138 --> 00:42:40,799
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, every time you go to work, or you have to hold your duty, that's all painful, that's all uncomfortable.

574
00:42:41,420 --> 00:42:47,701
[SPEAKER_02]: But you don't resist it, because you have a bigger picture in your mind, that this contributes towards that bigger picture, which is picture of pleasure.

575
00:42:48,682 --> 00:42:53,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Even being... even wanting the freedom from suffering is the picture of pleasure.

576
00:42:53,703 --> 00:42:55,144
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's just a natural principle.

577
00:42:55,184 --> 00:42:55,904
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how it works.

578
00:42:58,527 --> 00:43:00,168
[SPEAKER_02]: So, suffering is not in the pain.

579
00:43:00,668 --> 00:43:03,370
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if it were so, there would be no self-awareness of it.

580
00:43:03,430 --> 00:43:08,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Whenever there's pain, there's suffering, absence of pain, absence of suffering, absence of liability to suffer as well.

581
00:43:09,394 --> 00:43:10,974
[SPEAKER_02]: And you would not have a recognition of it.

582
00:43:13,716 --> 00:43:16,438
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact is that suffering is in your craving towards pain.

583
00:43:16,598 --> 00:43:24,302
[SPEAKER_02]: See, if I tell you if you endure this pain for 10 minutes and then you have a exponential reward, you won't crave against the pain.

584
00:43:24,363 --> 00:43:26,524
[SPEAKER_02]: You will want that pain, because you want the reward.

585
00:43:27,687 --> 00:43:29,608
[SPEAKER_02]: And you won't suffer in regard to the pain.

586
00:43:30,449 --> 00:43:33,490
[SPEAKER_02]: Although, you would suffer if I tell you, I just tricked you, I'm not giving you the reward.

587
00:43:34,751 --> 00:43:42,396
[SPEAKER_02]: And, without inflicting any physical pain on you, and you would suffer on account of it, more than you suffer on physical pain being inflicted upon you.

588
00:43:43,636 --> 00:43:43,816
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

589
00:43:43,876 --> 00:43:47,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Because your craving determines the suffering.

590
00:43:47,198 --> 00:43:48,739
[SPEAKER_02]: First, you were not craving against it.

591
00:43:50,280 --> 00:43:52,862
[SPEAKER_02]: You were not craving against the pain, but you were very much craving for

592
00:43:53,722 --> 00:43:54,682
[SPEAKER_02]: The great reward.

593
00:43:55,423 --> 00:43:57,684
[SPEAKER_02]: So, craving is present in regard to the reward.

594
00:43:58,224 --> 00:43:59,424
[SPEAKER_02]: I tell you there is no reward.

595
00:43:59,684 --> 00:44:00,585
[SPEAKER_02]: Your craving is there.

596
00:44:00,845 --> 00:44:02,205
[SPEAKER_02]: You suffer because of your craving.

597
00:44:03,726 --> 00:44:05,787
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to protect your reward because of your craving.

598
00:44:06,367 --> 00:44:07,107
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to defend it.

599
00:44:07,628 --> 00:44:13,070
[SPEAKER_02]: You will cry on account of it if you lose it because of the craving.

600
00:44:13,090 --> 00:44:15,751
[SPEAKER_02]: So, pain is the problem because of your craving.

601
00:44:15,831 --> 00:44:17,132
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, pain is not a problem.

602
00:44:17,212 --> 00:44:18,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Your craving is the problem.

603
00:44:19,512 --> 00:44:20,433
[SPEAKER_02]: But saying that now,

604
00:44:21,174 --> 00:44:27,100
[SPEAKER_02]: For somebody who is not free from craving, saying, okay, so pain is not the problem, well, that's an idea.

605
00:44:29,022 --> 00:44:35,509
[SPEAKER_02]: But if that idea is not freeing you from suffering, as in not freeing you from craving, means pain is the problem for you.

606
00:44:37,091 --> 00:44:40,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you don't know how not to crave, in regard to the pain.

607
00:44:45,775 --> 00:44:46,255
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

608
00:44:46,275 --> 00:44:52,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes people get a hint that, oh, suffering is not in the pain, but then overly accept any feeling that comes their way.

609
00:44:54,759 --> 00:44:56,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so, this is just so.

610
00:44:57,100 --> 00:44:58,200
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no problem in this.

611
00:44:58,740 --> 00:44:59,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Just accept it.

612
00:44:59,481 --> 00:45:00,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Just let it go.

613
00:45:02,322 --> 00:45:06,083
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you let go of you, of your idea of letting go?

614
00:45:07,264 --> 00:45:08,384
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you really let go of that?

615
00:45:09,045 --> 00:45:10,765
[SPEAKER_02]: You see, I'm letting go of all of this.

616
00:45:11,586 --> 00:45:14,587
[SPEAKER_02]: It still allows me to have at least some sense of control.

617
00:45:15,855 --> 00:45:17,155
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just practicing letting go.

618
00:45:19,416 --> 00:45:22,876
[SPEAKER_02]: A sense of control is a sense of craving.

619
00:45:24,377 --> 00:45:27,277
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're letting go of your cravings, with your craving.

620
00:45:29,218 --> 00:45:33,198
[SPEAKER_02]: And usually, those people get very upset, if that view is challenged.

621
00:45:33,779 --> 00:45:43,040
[SPEAKER_02]: They might not get upset on account of things they're letting go, but if the view of that letting go is challenged, they get upset, they defend it, because their view is in their control.

622
00:45:44,161 --> 00:45:45,441
[SPEAKER_02]: And on account of that, they suffer.

623
00:45:46,700 --> 00:45:50,741
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not an accurate application of your effort, in other words.

624
00:45:51,481 --> 00:45:58,723
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to apply your effort whereby what you are letting go is resulting in freedom from suffering in every regard.

625
00:46:01,404 --> 00:46:11,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just in this particular little corner it works, but if anything else changes, if somebody challenges it, I'll be affected, I'll be emotionally disturbed on account of it.

626
00:46:29,458 --> 00:46:30,759
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how would you not crave for pain?

627
00:46:35,843 --> 00:46:37,264
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what liability to pain is.

628
00:46:37,584 --> 00:46:40,106
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what liability to suffering is.

629
00:46:40,246 --> 00:46:43,508
[SPEAKER_02]: It's inability to not crave.

630
00:46:44,069 --> 00:46:45,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Just think of the stock answer.

631
00:46:45,870 --> 00:46:46,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

632
00:46:46,251 --> 00:46:47,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandon craving.

633
00:46:48,692 --> 00:46:48,852
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

634
00:46:48,893 --> 00:46:53,376
[SPEAKER_02]: So, craving is not a matter of choice.

635
00:46:54,897 --> 00:46:57,319
[SPEAKER_02]: But, at the same time, it's something you're responsible for.

636
00:47:03,515 --> 00:47:06,617
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as I'm not an arahant, I'm responsible for craving.

637
00:47:07,377 --> 00:47:08,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

638
00:47:08,218 --> 00:47:13,180
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it arises... Well, you're never free from craving.

639
00:47:13,540 --> 00:47:16,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you always feel something, which means you always crave something in regard to feeling.

640
00:47:17,823 --> 00:47:20,664
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why craving is not in regard to... only in regard to pain.

641
00:47:21,264 --> 00:47:23,245
[SPEAKER_02]: It's in regard to the domain of whatever's felt.

642
00:47:25,266 --> 00:47:28,528
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you feel pleasant, you will automatically crave towards it.

643
00:47:29,309 --> 00:47:31,770
[SPEAKER_02]: If you feel unpleasant, you will automatically crave away from it.

644
00:47:33,026 --> 00:47:38,747
[SPEAKER_02]: If you feel neither, well, you will automatically crave towards distracting yourself from it.

645
00:47:42,088 --> 00:47:44,309
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you stop that automatic craving?

646
00:47:45,029 --> 00:47:46,269
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you are responsible for it.

647
00:47:47,309 --> 00:47:48,790
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is not part of the aggregate.

648
00:47:48,850 --> 00:47:50,670
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not part of the things that arise for you.

649
00:47:51,370 --> 00:47:52,770
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your attitude towards it.

650
00:47:55,211 --> 00:47:59,152
[SPEAKER_00]: By stopping... refusing to... stopping...

651
00:48:03,206 --> 00:48:08,607
[SPEAKER_02]: So, but why would you want to stop the denial of feeling?

652
00:48:09,527 --> 00:48:13,008
[SPEAKER_02]: What would be your motivation behind that?

653
00:48:13,028 --> 00:48:15,688
[SPEAKER_00]: To deny it.

654
00:48:15,828 --> 00:48:19,949
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to stop the denial.

655
00:48:20,029 --> 00:48:22,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Your wanting to stop denial is rooted in denying the craving.

656
00:48:25,030 --> 00:48:26,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Because how are you affected by craving?

657
00:48:27,091 --> 00:48:28,491
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said, craving doesn't arise.

658
00:48:29,372 --> 00:48:33,633
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, feelings arise for you.

659
00:48:33,653 --> 00:48:34,653
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is there to stop?

660
00:48:41,835 --> 00:48:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have an idea on this?

661
00:48:44,216 --> 00:48:46,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, your understanding, your attitude towards it.

662
00:48:47,037 --> 00:48:47,777
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was thinking...

663
00:48:47,797 --> 00:48:48,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards what?

664
00:48:49,438 --> 00:48:50,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, towards the order.

665
00:48:51,878 --> 00:48:52,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I was thinking.

666
00:48:52,678 --> 00:48:52,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Go on.

667
00:48:53,119 --> 00:48:56,279
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Let's stick with the feeling and craving.

668
00:48:56,580 --> 00:48:57,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

669
00:48:57,200 --> 00:48:57,980
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

670
00:49:01,043 --> 00:49:04,704
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't attend to craving.

671
00:49:04,724 --> 00:49:06,684
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is not an object of your mind.

672
00:49:06,744 --> 00:49:07,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I see my craving.

673
00:49:07,925 --> 00:49:14,526
[SPEAKER_02]: But for as long as you think that you can control... Craving is peripheral in regard to whatever is presently felt.

674
00:49:14,906 --> 00:49:16,066
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

675
00:49:16,106 --> 00:49:27,749
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you keep the understanding that you can affect the feeling, you can change it, you have a right to...

676
00:49:29,507 --> 00:49:30,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Not want this feeling to be there.

677
00:49:31,208 --> 00:49:31,668
[SPEAKER_02]: Right, yeah.

678
00:49:31,828 --> 00:49:33,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Because those are two different things, by the way.

679
00:49:33,849 --> 00:49:34,929
[SPEAKER_02]: You can change your feeling.

680
00:49:36,030 --> 00:49:37,471
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, sensuality has effect.

681
00:49:38,511 --> 00:49:38,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

682
00:49:38,791 --> 00:49:39,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Ill-will has effect.

683
00:49:40,132 --> 00:49:40,492
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

684
00:49:40,872 --> 00:49:43,753
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, oh, it's completely has nothing to do with anything.

685
00:49:43,833 --> 00:49:44,294
[SPEAKER_02]: It works.

686
00:49:44,654 --> 00:49:44,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

687
00:49:45,194 --> 00:49:47,615
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the only way that Puthujjana knows of working.

688
00:49:49,756 --> 00:49:53,279
[SPEAKER_02]: But the thing you refer to is like, you have a right to do so.

689
00:49:53,319 --> 00:49:54,140
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a different thing.

690
00:49:54,920 --> 00:49:56,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already on the level of the view.

691
00:49:57,562 --> 00:50:03,427
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a right to interfere with this feeling, which means I have a right to crave against the feeling or for the feeling.

692
00:50:03,887 --> 00:50:10,112
[SPEAKER_02]: And for as long as you don't abandon the view that you have a right to do so, you will be automatically doing it so.

693
00:50:11,553 --> 00:50:15,997
[SPEAKER_02]: So, only through going against the grain of that view, that I have a right,

694
00:50:17,186 --> 00:50:18,186
[SPEAKER_02]: You will stop doing it.

695
00:50:18,306 --> 00:50:21,427
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you stop that automatic response of craving.

696
00:50:21,967 --> 00:50:25,028
[SPEAKER_02]: By ceasing to provide maintenance for it.

697
00:50:26,249 --> 00:50:32,751
[SPEAKER_02]: And this view that you have a right to do so, is how you maintain the attitude of craving of doing so.

698
00:50:36,772 --> 00:50:43,254
[SPEAKER_02]: So, abandon the view that you have a right towards pleasurable feeling, towards unpleasant feeling, towards neutral feeling.

699
00:50:44,932 --> 00:50:45,773
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have a right.

700
00:50:46,153 --> 00:50:48,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Whichever feeling is there, you are subjected to it.

701
00:50:48,175 --> 00:50:49,236
[SPEAKER_02]: You have no rights over it.

702
00:50:50,036 --> 00:50:50,717
[SPEAKER_01]: You're within it.

703
00:50:50,897 --> 00:50:52,699
[SPEAKER_01]: You're sub... yeah, that's what I want to say.

704
00:50:52,719 --> 00:50:54,840
[SPEAKER_01]: You're... you are kind of subordinate to that feeling.

705
00:50:54,860 --> 00:50:55,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

706
00:50:55,641 --> 00:50:56,462
[SPEAKER_02]: And that cannot change.

707
00:50:56,962 --> 00:51:00,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Any change that you can inflict, so to speak, is within.

708
00:51:00,525 --> 00:51:00,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

709
00:51:01,726 --> 00:51:02,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Is within that...

710
00:51:02,347 --> 00:51:03,127
[SPEAKER_02]: The feeling that is enduring.

711
00:51:03,187 --> 00:51:04,428
[SPEAKER_02]: ...which fully contains you.

712
00:51:04,448 --> 00:51:04,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

713
00:51:04,889 --> 00:51:05,849
[SPEAKER_02]: That you have to endure.

714
00:51:07,471 --> 00:51:08,872
[SPEAKER_02]: So, stop giving in.

715
00:51:10,255 --> 00:51:12,956
[SPEAKER_02]: To the means of changing feeling on account of what's felt.

716
00:51:13,796 --> 00:51:15,876
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you will stop perpetuating craving.

717
00:51:16,977 --> 00:51:17,677
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't understand that.

718
00:51:17,697 --> 00:51:22,638
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not something, oh, here is a recipe, just stick to these rules and these methods.

719
00:51:22,698 --> 00:51:23,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

720
00:51:23,298 --> 00:51:31,580
[SPEAKER_02]: Because different, literally, different whenever you become recollect of the present moment, there is a feeling enduring there, which means there's going to be an attitude towards it.

721
00:51:32,161 --> 00:51:34,441
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the attitude you need to be dealing with.

722
00:51:34,561 --> 00:51:37,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the theory of the attitude that I must deal with.

723
00:51:39,131 --> 00:51:43,378
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what are you feeling right here, right now?

724
00:51:43,418 --> 00:51:43,879
[SPEAKER_02]: Neutral.

725
00:51:44,560 --> 00:51:46,083
[SPEAKER_02]: What is... are you... are you aware of it?

726
00:51:46,163 --> 00:51:47,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you losing the sight of that neutral?

727
00:51:47,886 --> 00:51:49,829
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you being distracted towards other things?

728
00:51:50,310 --> 00:51:51,252
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your work, then.

729
00:51:52,648 --> 00:51:53,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I feel unpleasant.

730
00:51:54,229 --> 00:51:58,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there an implicit attitude that I should not feel this?

731
00:51:58,271 --> 00:51:59,732
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a right to not feel this.

732
00:51:59,772 --> 00:52:01,132
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm entitled to not feel this.

733
00:52:01,672 --> 00:52:03,173
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't wait for it to go away.

734
00:52:03,854 --> 00:52:09,496
[SPEAKER_02]: All these thoughts are exemplification of the attitude that I am first and this is just here temporarily.

735
00:52:09,536 --> 00:52:11,057
[SPEAKER_02]: I must get rid of it one way or the other.

736
00:52:11,197 --> 00:52:13,398
[SPEAKER_02]: Even I will endure it for the rest of my life.

737
00:52:13,618 --> 00:52:13,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

738
00:52:14,579 --> 00:52:16,040
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, because I don't want it to be here.

739
00:52:18,270 --> 00:52:18,630
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that.

740
00:52:19,151 --> 00:52:20,611
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not what you do on account of it.

741
00:52:21,172 --> 00:52:22,072
[SPEAKER_02]: It's whether you feel...

742
00:52:22,413 --> 00:52:22,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

743
00:52:22,853 --> 00:52:23,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

744
00:52:24,014 --> 00:52:29,977
[SPEAKER_02]: Why you're doing it, which will reveal whether you feel entitled to get rid of it, or have it, or whatever.

745
00:52:30,257 --> 00:52:32,099
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will, for as long as you're not aware of it.

746
00:52:33,860 --> 00:52:35,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can act on account of it, but...

747
00:52:37,502 --> 00:52:46,394
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a difference between acting on account of it, under the impression that I am in control of it, and under the impression that I'm not in control.

748
00:52:46,454 --> 00:52:51,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not my right to be... Well, it depends what acting on account of it means.

749
00:52:51,961 --> 00:52:52,862
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's skillfully.

750
00:52:54,298 --> 00:52:58,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Not... like if it's not against the precepts, not against the training.

751
00:52:59,019 --> 00:53:00,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Right, right, right, right, right.

752
00:53:00,120 --> 00:53:00,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

753
00:53:00,680 --> 00:53:01,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the thing.

754
00:53:01,660 --> 00:53:06,621
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's not against the precepts, if it's not against the training, you're not really acting out of it.

755
00:53:07,282 --> 00:53:13,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Acting out of it means acting for the purpose of either getting more of this feeling or getting rid of this feeling.

756
00:53:14,524 --> 00:53:19,605
[SPEAKER_02]: But, that only applies on the level of the body, and that's not where the feeling is rooted.

757
00:53:21,410 --> 00:53:25,734
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to start by not acting out of it, as in not going against the precepts.

758
00:53:26,294 --> 00:53:33,960
[SPEAKER_02]: But then... See, it's not against the precepts to go alone and sit... be alone in your kuti and sit quietly.

759
00:53:35,382 --> 00:53:38,744
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not on the level of unwholesomeness of breaking the precepts.

760
00:53:39,225 --> 00:53:42,948
[SPEAKER_02]: But you might be well doing it for very unwholesome reasons.

761
00:53:42,968 --> 00:53:45,470
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't just say, oh, it's just a question of not acting out of it.

762
00:53:47,313 --> 00:53:58,741
[SPEAKER_02]: Not acting out of it first, to contain yourself, but then it's a question of understanding whatever current feeling... whatever current feeling is and whatever your attitude towards it is.

763
00:53:59,622 --> 00:54:07,428
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you might be on paper wholesome because you're always secluded in your kuti and so on, but the true reason for that might be because you hate everybody else.

764
00:54:08,168 --> 00:54:09,289
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't stand other people.

765
00:54:09,389 --> 00:54:10,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is a disturbance.

766
00:54:11,431 --> 00:54:14,333
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you just want to find the environment that's in your control.

767
00:54:14,933 --> 00:54:15,454
[SPEAKER_02]: My kuti.

768
00:54:16,612 --> 00:54:18,713
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody comes and tells you, get out of there, could you?

769
00:54:19,693 --> 00:54:22,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Would you remain indifferent and equanimous?

770
00:54:23,975 --> 00:54:27,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would you be disturbed?

771
00:54:27,796 --> 00:54:32,598
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what you need to do is know the motivation in regard to your present enduring feeling.

772
00:54:35,039 --> 00:54:36,079
[SPEAKER_02]: And not lose the sight of it.

773
00:54:38,300 --> 00:54:38,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Ever.

774
00:54:54,588 --> 00:54:56,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's where acting out truly is.

775
00:54:57,730 --> 00:55:02,334
[SPEAKER_02]: In not... in ignoring that degree of that motivation behind.

776
00:55:07,738 --> 00:55:10,020
[SPEAKER_02]: Because as I said, I will not act out of my feeling.

777
00:55:10,220 --> 00:55:10,501
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

778
00:55:11,581 --> 00:55:12,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, because I want to get rid of it.

779
00:55:13,783 --> 00:55:18,247
[SPEAKER_02]: So now, for me, getting rid of the feeling is abstaining from getting rid of the feeling.

780
00:55:23,791 --> 00:55:24,851
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can never...

781
00:55:24,872 --> 00:55:26,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, always look at your motivation.

782
00:55:26,832 --> 00:55:27,493
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your method.

783
00:55:28,153 --> 00:55:31,154
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I suppose you can say that, but... Why do I want to do that?

784
00:55:31,374 --> 00:55:31,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

785
00:55:32,215 --> 00:55:33,075
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why am I doing that?

786
00:55:33,275 --> 00:55:39,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, always has to be discerned on the basis of a recollected present experience as a whole.

787
00:55:39,698 --> 00:55:41,019
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's where the motivation is.

788
00:55:41,099 --> 00:55:42,319
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the feeling is induced.

789
00:55:42,379 --> 00:55:43,220
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the moods are.

790
00:55:48,222 --> 00:55:50,203
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now, while you're trying to figure out what I'm saying,

791
00:55:51,135 --> 00:55:51,375
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

792
00:55:51,655 --> 00:55:52,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Ask yourself.

793
00:55:52,176 --> 00:55:52,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

794
00:55:52,416 --> 00:55:54,477
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why is this important to be understood?

795
00:55:55,098 --> 00:55:56,779
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you'll understand what I'm saying.

796
00:55:57,900 --> 00:55:59,621
[SPEAKER_00]: To be free from suffering.

797
00:55:59,681 --> 00:56:00,502
[SPEAKER_00]: To be free from...

798
00:56:00,842 --> 00:56:01,042
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

799
00:56:01,142 --> 00:56:03,404
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why do you want to be free from suffering now?

800
00:56:04,665 --> 00:56:09,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it because that already implies you're not free?

801
00:56:09,228 --> 00:56:10,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It implies that I'm not free from suffering.

802
00:56:11,169 --> 00:56:16,413
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how does that implication manifest in your experience?

803
00:56:18,592 --> 00:56:19,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're not free from suffering.

804
00:56:20,032 --> 00:56:20,393
[SPEAKER_00]: How's that?

805
00:56:20,413 --> 00:56:21,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Because feeling is felt.

806
00:56:22,053 --> 00:56:22,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

807
00:56:22,273 --> 00:56:22,913
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how is that?

808
00:56:23,053 --> 00:56:24,334
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'm not free from suffering.

809
00:56:24,374 --> 00:56:25,514
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that a concern for you?

810
00:56:26,734 --> 00:56:27,835
[SPEAKER_02]: Is concern unpleasant?

811
00:56:28,375 --> 00:56:28,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

812
00:56:29,315 --> 00:56:31,316
[SPEAKER_02]: Is displeasure a problem?

813
00:56:32,356 --> 00:56:35,777
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this... Is pain a problem?

814
00:56:38,278 --> 00:56:38,758
[SPEAKER_00]: A craving.

815
00:56:39,878 --> 00:56:40,618
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

816
00:56:40,838 --> 00:56:42,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving is a problem in regard to pain.

817
00:56:42,739 --> 00:56:45,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, you can't have craving without the pain or without a feeling.

818
00:56:47,072 --> 00:56:59,903
[SPEAKER_02]: So, through going through this, basically, almost like drills of reflection, you keep hammering in the context that pain while endures, or feeling while it endures, it's not a problem, but there is no problem without the feeling.

819
00:57:01,905 --> 00:57:06,509
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you get to then discern the aptitude, peripherally, to the problem of the feeling.

820
00:57:07,930 --> 00:57:10,693
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I meant, upādāna is not in the five aggregates.

821
00:57:11,694 --> 00:57:14,056
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is no upādāna without the five aggregates being there.

822
00:57:15,078 --> 00:57:23,823
[SPEAKER_02]: But usually, when people think about holding and attachment, whatever you want to translate it, it just becomes another thing, another thing I can attend to, focus on, say no to, choose it.

823
00:57:24,483 --> 00:57:24,643
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

824
00:57:24,943 --> 00:57:28,245
[SPEAKER_02]: You can do that with things that aggregates present you, but upādāna is not in them.

825
00:57:29,246 --> 00:57:31,847
[SPEAKER_02]: Nowhere, in nothing, not in between, not in response, nothing.

826
00:57:33,048 --> 00:57:35,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, without aggregates, there is no upādāna.

827
00:57:38,230 --> 00:57:40,151
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you look at the aggregates, you're not gonna see upādāna.

828
00:57:40,572 --> 00:57:43,053
[SPEAKER_02]: It's completely peripheral from your look of the aggregates.

829
00:57:44,984 --> 00:57:45,705
[SPEAKER_02]: Same with the feeling.

830
00:57:45,805 --> 00:57:46,306
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

831
00:57:46,326 --> 00:57:47,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can't see your craving.

832
00:57:47,788 --> 00:57:50,231
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't say no to your craving, because that's not craving.

833
00:57:50,351 --> 00:57:51,112
[SPEAKER_02]: You're craving that.

834
00:57:53,675 --> 00:57:55,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is peripheral to present enduring feeling.

835
00:57:56,648 --> 00:57:59,791
[SPEAKER_02]: So, discern, recollect, present enduring feeling, as I said.

836
00:58:00,131 --> 00:58:03,994
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, suffering is... not being in control is a problem.

837
00:58:04,014 --> 00:58:04,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

838
00:58:04,254 --> 00:58:05,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because I'm liable to it.

839
00:58:05,655 --> 00:58:07,237
[SPEAKER_02]: It implies I can suffer.

840
00:58:07,617 --> 00:58:08,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, how is that?

841
00:58:08,257 --> 00:58:09,759
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, that's felt as a concern.

842
00:58:10,119 --> 00:58:10,920
[SPEAKER_02]: What is a concern?

843
00:58:11,160 --> 00:58:14,683
[SPEAKER_02]: A concern is an unpleasant feeling that endures.

844
00:58:16,564 --> 00:58:17,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, so I'm craving against the...

845
00:58:18,225 --> 00:58:23,810
[SPEAKER_02]: I wouldn't have a problem with concern if by some whatever reason it's not felt unpleasantly.

846
00:58:25,895 --> 00:58:33,721
[SPEAKER_02]: Any problem you have in the world, any problem you had, you would have, you will have, it's only a problem for one reason.

847
00:58:35,423 --> 00:58:42,228
[SPEAKER_02]: It's felt unpleasantly and you not crave against it.

848
00:58:42,248 --> 00:58:43,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, concern is not a problem.

849
00:58:43,449 --> 00:58:44,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Anxiety is not a problem.

850
00:58:45,010 --> 00:58:46,031
[SPEAKER_02]: None of these things are a problem.

851
00:58:48,713 --> 00:58:51,395
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain is the problem, because you cannot... you don't know how not to

852
00:58:52,633 --> 00:58:57,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are just symptoms, or... Those are just further results.

853
00:58:57,936 --> 00:58:58,196
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

854
00:59:03,239 --> 00:59:05,521
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we realize the pain is not the problem either.

855
00:59:07,602 --> 00:59:08,923
[SPEAKER_02]: It's my craving that's the problem.

856
00:59:09,023 --> 00:59:09,743
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I suffer.

857
00:59:10,144 --> 00:59:10,804
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what Buddha said.

858
00:59:11,364 --> 00:59:13,385
[SPEAKER_02]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

859
00:59:13,526 --> 00:59:14,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Absence of craving, absence of suffering.

860
00:59:16,427 --> 00:59:17,388
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's simultaneous.

861
00:59:18,499 --> 00:59:24,982
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whenever there is a suffering present in me, means craving is present in me, to the extent that there is suffering.

862
00:59:27,643 --> 00:59:31,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Whenever there is no suffering present in me, then there is no craving present in me.

863
00:59:36,803 --> 00:59:40,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually, people regard it in the linear sense of craving is a matter of choice.

864
00:59:41,344 --> 00:59:48,506
[SPEAKER_02]: You just say no to it, say no to it, and somehow down the line, your feelings will wither away, and then you'll be free from everything.

865
00:59:49,326 --> 00:59:49,486
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

866
00:59:49,506 --> 00:59:53,467
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're craving for that, because you're unaware of your motivation in regard to why you're doing it in the first place.

867
00:59:56,728 --> 01:00:00,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, when I asked you the other day in that talk, are you liable to suffering?

868
01:00:00,209 --> 01:00:00,389
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

869
01:00:00,469 --> 01:00:01,309
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is that a problem?

870
01:00:02,230 --> 01:00:03,090
[SPEAKER_02]: How does that feel?

871
01:00:04,594 --> 01:00:06,555
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's like, oh, I feel such a... right here, right now.

872
01:00:07,515 --> 01:00:17,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, so, if you don't feel suffering on account of right here, right now, liability to suffering, would you then suffer on account of anything that can come through that liability?

873
01:00:17,238 --> 01:00:21,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

874
01:00:21,519 --> 01:00:22,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you uprooted the feeling.

875
01:00:22,739 --> 01:00:28,001
[SPEAKER_01]: The recognition of my liability to suffering is not a problem anymore.

876
01:00:28,321 --> 01:00:28,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

877
01:00:28,601 --> 01:00:29,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it a problem?

878
01:00:29,881 --> 01:00:31,462
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason this is a problem is because

879
01:00:32,640 --> 01:00:37,042
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know the escape out of suffering, which is another way of saying, I don't know how to stop craving.

880
01:00:38,362 --> 01:00:39,562
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the Four Noble Truths is.

881
01:00:39,622 --> 01:00:46,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you understand what the true problem is, which is not the pain, it's the craving in regard... that's where the problem is.

882
01:00:47,005 --> 01:00:49,846
[SPEAKER_02]: You then understand that absence of that means absence of the problem.

883
01:00:50,386 --> 01:00:57,309
[SPEAKER_02]: Regardless, even if the people come and, you know, start chopping your limbs and abusing you and everything else, you will not suffer, because suffering was never in those things.

884
01:00:57,469 --> 01:00:58,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering is not in the aggregates.

885
01:00:59,609 --> 01:01:01,410
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, upādāna is not in the aggregates.

886
01:01:33,806 --> 01:01:41,849
[SPEAKER_02]: You can even ask yourself, why is the... why is the... why is the pain of interrogation unpleasant?

887
01:01:43,569 --> 01:01:44,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is it unpleasant?

888
01:01:47,871 --> 01:01:49,411
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you came against the pain of it.

889
01:01:50,372 --> 01:01:52,552
[SPEAKER_02]: Because... You crave against the pain of it.

890
01:01:54,513 --> 01:01:56,694
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's suffering because you crave against it.

891
01:01:57,574 --> 01:01:59,054
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because in itself it's suffering.

892
01:02:01,115 --> 01:02:01,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

893
01:02:01,495 --> 01:02:03,216
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess, and there's also the motivation

894
01:02:06,438 --> 01:02:14,761
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you have to start like that.

895
01:02:14,961 --> 01:02:15,681
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what Buddha meant.

896
01:02:17,702 --> 01:02:19,542
[SPEAKER_02]: What was it?

897
01:02:19,763 --> 01:02:22,884
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving, conceit by the means of conceit.

898
01:02:25,305 --> 01:02:27,385
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you crave now to be free from craving.

899
01:02:30,286 --> 01:02:31,427
[SPEAKER_02]: As a result of that craving,

900
01:02:32,358 --> 01:02:40,185
[SPEAKER_02]: You will hopefully do the right... like, understand the right things, make the right effort, that will then result in cancelling that very craving.

901
01:02:41,726 --> 01:02:43,007
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly what I'm describing.

902
01:02:43,027 --> 01:03:01,342
[SPEAKER_02]: So, because you want to be free from suffering, you start to question the nature of the feeling, and then you get to realize that suffering is not in the feeling, it's in the attitude in regard to that feeling, and then you get to realize that that attitude is not a matter of your choice, but more like a matter of your secondary choices that result in feeding that attitude.

903
01:03:02,616 --> 01:03:05,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Choices towards pleasure, choices towards no pain, acting out of it.

904
01:03:06,099 --> 01:03:08,781
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you stop doing that and you stop feeding the suffering.

905
01:03:09,241 --> 01:03:10,262
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeding the craving, sorry.

906
01:03:12,724 --> 01:03:14,385
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you destroy the craving.

907
01:03:14,706 --> 01:03:15,787
[SPEAKER_02]: On account of the very craving.

908
01:03:16,187 --> 01:03:18,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeding the craving is feeding the suffering.

909
01:03:18,309 --> 01:03:19,089
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, of course.

910
01:03:20,130 --> 01:03:21,451
[SPEAKER_00]: That's literally synonymous.

911
01:03:27,296 --> 01:03:27,756
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving?

912
01:03:29,057 --> 01:03:32,360
[SPEAKER_02]: Endured presence of craving, peripherally, as the attitude of your mind?

913
01:03:33,058 --> 01:03:34,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Attitude of craving is felt as suffering.

914
01:03:35,320 --> 01:03:36,021
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same thing.

915
01:03:37,323 --> 01:03:39,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, oh, there is craving and then there is suffering.

916
01:03:40,546 --> 01:03:42,508
[SPEAKER_02]: There's one thing.

917
01:03:42,568 --> 01:03:44,490
[SPEAKER_02]: Attitude of craving that's felt suffering.

918
01:03:45,451 --> 01:03:45,952
[SPEAKER_02]: As suffering.

919
01:03:47,894 --> 01:03:51,638
[SPEAKER_02]: So, removing the attitude of craving cannot anymore result in feeling suffering.

920
01:03:52,805 --> 01:03:57,969
[SPEAKER_02]: But the reason... see, it's so hard... well, it's so easy, because the attitude is invisible.

921
01:03:58,109 --> 01:04:00,091
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said, you can't attend to your craving.

922
01:04:00,191 --> 01:04:02,232
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't see, oh, this is my craving.

923
01:04:02,953 --> 01:04:03,994
[SPEAKER_02]: You only feel suffering.

924
01:04:04,494 --> 01:04:06,796
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, when you look, all you see is things in front of you.

925
01:04:07,036 --> 01:04:08,257
[SPEAKER_02]: I see this, I see that.

926
01:04:08,637 --> 01:04:13,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, natural response, response with the grain of that deception, is

927
01:04:14,842 --> 01:04:16,243
[SPEAKER_02]: I suffer because he said that.

928
01:04:16,783 --> 01:04:18,104
[SPEAKER_02]: I suffer because I lost this.

929
01:04:18,884 --> 01:04:19,345
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

930
01:04:19,765 --> 01:04:22,747
[SPEAKER_02]: There is losing of this and suffering simultaneously present.

931
01:04:23,927 --> 01:04:33,933
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's a mistake of the, like, fundamental proportion to assume that because they're simultaneously present, that's why this is suffering.

932
01:04:34,653 --> 01:04:35,714
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the nature of ignorance.

933
01:04:38,655 --> 01:04:43,498
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why even a vijja cannot be in the aggregates, cannot be in things you perceive or feel or intend.

934
01:04:44,641 --> 01:04:45,382
[SPEAKER_02]: It's inrigate.

935
01:04:46,483 --> 01:04:49,506
[SPEAKER_02]: But it cannot be found anywhere else, except where those things are.

936
01:04:50,627 --> 01:04:59,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how can you mistake your suffering for something you perceived, or even for the feeling you felt, if feeling and perception were not there?

937
01:05:00,518 --> 01:05:01,899
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to be mistaken with.

